Patch 45. In the series since 2026-10-17, after patch 44.

On Linux and Android, Memory::Initialize tried fixed bases (2^32 first) and mapped the guest
memory's views there with MAP_FIXED, without reserving the range first (MapFileView's comment
assumes a reservation, which only macOS made). MAP_FIXED replaces whatever is mapped: when address
space randomization had put another library's or allocator's memory in [base, base + 4.5 GB),
it silently became guest memory, and guest writes then corrupted it. On the Galaxy Tab S5e about
one start in two crashed in the GPU driver (libGLESv2_adreno.so) reading a pointer made of ASCII
text, at the first texture upload. Now the whole range is reserved without access first
(MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE; on kernels before 4.17 a reservation placed elsewhere also counts as taken,
patch 38), the views replace that reservation alone, and a taken range is logged with the
mapping that holds it ("Guest memory arena: 0x... is taken by ...") before the next base is
tried. The reservation keeps one page without access before and one after the views, so a host
write running off either end of guest memory faults at the writer. When no base is free, the
start fails with a message (before, the loop could end with no base and go on). When the views
fail part way at one base, the ones made stay in place until one call replaces them and the rest
of the reservation, which is then freed, so no hole opens for another thread to map into.

Desktop Linux: the same base as before whenever 2^32 is free (it was in all 101 desktop game logs
on this machine, 2026-10-17); otherwise the next free base, instead of mapping over what is there.

Test: tests/unit/memory/alloc_fixed_test.cpp, "The guest memory arena does not map over someone
else's mapping": a page mapped in the middle of the first free candidate range keeps its contents
and the arena goes elsewhere, and the pages before and after the views have no access. Without
the change the arena maps at that candidate and the page reads 0. Linux SDK ctest 1773 of 1773.
On the devices: the tablet's starts with the game, and the POCO X7 (docs/rexglue-upstream.md,
section 8).

